{"type": "final", "turn": 2, "content": "\n\nShort answer: **none**. At \u03b1 = 5% (and \u03b4 = 0.1), no part of the map clears the exact test with your 300 random labels.\n\n**Why:** the overall label count shows ~69/300 \u2248 23% of windows wrong, so even the most-confident regions fall far short of a 5% error cap:\n\n| Zone | Coverage | Labels inside | Wrong | Upper bound @ \u03b4=0.1 | Certified? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Most confident | 15% | 46 | 3 | 13.9% | \u2717 |\n| | 25% | 68 | 3 | 9.5% | \u2717 |\n| Whole map | 100% | 300 | 69 | 26.4% | \u2717 |\n\nEven the top-15% block (\u22482,372 windows) shows 3/46 \u2248 6.5% errors in-sample, with a 13.9% certified ceiling \u2014 well above 5%.\n\n**Options if you still want a certified zone:**\n1. **Loosen \u03b1** \u2014 e.g. \u03b1 = 15% would make the top-15% zone plausible (its upper bound is already under 15%); I can re-run `certify_zone` with that \u03b1.\n2. **Label more** \u2014 only 300 random labels were used; a larger budget tightens the bounds (certification needs \u226545 labels inside the zone, which you have, but the observed rates are simply too high).\n3. **Accept uncertified use** \u2014 treat the top-confidence areas as \"most reliable\" descriptively, without a 5% guarantee.\n\nTell me which you'd like (e.g. \"try \u03b1=15%\") and I'll rerun.", "seq": 2, "t": 23.161, "seconds": 13.725}
